//BMM150 3-Axis Magnetometer Interface for Raspberry Pi
This repository contains the hardware documentation and Python implementation for interfacing a BMM150 3-axis magnetometer with a Raspberry Pi over I2C. 
The script handles sensor wake-up, continuous measurement mode configuration, and bitwise reconstruction of the raw X, Y, and Z magnetic field data.

//Hardware Requirements
Raspberry Pi (Tested on standard 40-pin GPIO headers)
BMM150 Magnetometer Breakout Board
Female-to-Female Jumper Wires

//Wiring Configuration
Ensure the Raspberry Pi is completely powered off and unplugged before making these connections to prevent electrical shorts.

//BMM150 Pin      Wire Color      Raspberry Pi GPIO Header      Pin Number
VCC (3.3V)	      Purple	       3V3 Power	                  Pin 1
GND	              Orange	       Ground	                      Pin 6
SDA (Data)	      Blue	         GPIO 2 (SDA)	                Pin 3
SCL (Clock)	      Yellow	       GPIO 3 (SCL)	                Pin 5
INT	              Green	         Leave Disconnected	          N/A

//System Setup
1. Enable I2C on the Raspberry Pi
Open a terminal (or connect via SSH) and launch the configuration tool:

sudo raspi-config

Navigate to Interfacing Options > I2C and select Yes to enable the ARM I2C interface.

2. Install Dependencies
Update your package list and install the I2C diagnostic tools alongside the Python SMBus library:

sudo apt update
sudo apt install i2c-tools python3-smbus

3. Verify Hardware Connection
Run an I2C bus scan to confirm the Pi detects the sensor:

i2cdetect -y 1

You should see 13 (0x13) appear in the output grid, which is the default I2C address for the BMM150.

//Installation and Usage
1. Clone this repository to your Raspberry Pi workspace:

git clone <your-repository-url>
cd <your-repository-name>

2. Create the main Python script read_compass.py:
import smbus
import time

bus = smbus.SMBus(1)
BMM150_ADDR = 0x13

# BMM150 Control Registers
POWER_CONTROL_REG = 0x4B
OP_MODE_REG = 0x4C
DATA_X_LSB = 0x42

def setup_sensor():
    # Wake up from Suspend mode (Power Control bit 0 = 1)
    bus.write_byte_data(BMM150_ADDR, POWER_CONTROL_REG, 0x01)
    time.sleep(0.05)
    
    # Set to Normal mode for continuous measurement
    bus.write_byte_data(BMM150_ADDR, OP_MODE_REG, 0x00)
    time.sleep(0.05)

def read_raw_data():
    # Read 6 bytes of data starting from X_LSB (0x42)
    data = bus.read_i2c_block_data(BMM150_ADDR, DATA_X_LSB, 6)
    
    # Reconstruct 13-bit (X/Y) and 15-bit (Z) values
    x = ((data[1] << 8) | data[0]) >> 3
    y = ((data[3] << 8) | data[2]) >> 3
    z = ((data[5] << 8) | data[4]) >> 1
    
    # Apply two's complement for negative values
    if x > 4095: x -= 8192
    if y > 4095: y -= 8192
    if z > 16383: z -= 32768
        
    return x, y, z

if __name__ == "__main__":
    setup_sensor()
    print("Reading magnetometer data... Press Ctrl+C to stop.")
    
    try:
        while True:
            x, y, z = read_raw_data()
            print(f"X: {x:6d} | Y: {y:6d} | Z: {z:6d}")
            time.sleep(0.2)
            
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        print("\nStopped by user.")
        # Return sensor to sleep mode
        bus.write_byte_data(BMM150_ADDR, POWER_CONTROL_REG, 0x00)


//Execute the following script:

python3 read_compass.py
